<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Britons (Celtic people)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ancient Britons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Britons were the Celtic-speaking inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> through the Roman period and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. They spoke Brittonic languages, the ancestors of Welsh, Cornish and Breton, and were organised in peoples such as the Catuvellauni and Iceni. After the Roman withdrawal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement, Britons kept independent kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and the north, and some migrated to Brittany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people.jpg" alt="Britons (Celtic people)" width="220" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: malus36 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Gaels_Brythons_Picts_GB.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>There is no consensus on when Celtic languages reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The traditional view derived Celtic culture from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, while John Koch and Barry Cunliffe proposed that Celtic developed as a language of trade in the Atlantic Bronze Age zone, and Patrick Sims-Williams that it arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. A study of 2021 found a migration into southern Britain from 1300 to 800 BCE, genetically close to ancient people of Gaul, which the authors called a plausible vector for early Celtic languages. The earliest mention of the inhabitants is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas</a>, around 320 BCE, who calls them Pretanoi and says that they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> consisted of many tribes and kingdoms associated with hillforts, and the Britons followed a Celtic religion overseen by druids. Some southern polities had strong links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and minted their own coins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> invaded in 43 and by 84 had decisively conquered southern Britain. Hadrian's Wall was fortified in 122, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> was built from 142 but abandoned after about twenty years. The Britons south of the wall mostly kept their land under Roman governors. Roman rule ended about 410.</p><p>Some fifty years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> began settling the south-eastern coast. Many Brittonic kingdoms disappeared over the following centuries: Ceint, the kingdom of Kent, fell in 456 and Elmet in 627. Britons colonised Brittany and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>, and founded Britonia in Gallaecia in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In the north Gaelic speakers from Ireland settled in the west. Some northern kingdoms resisted: Rheged survived into the 8th century and Gododdin until about 775, and Ystrad Clud, Strathclyde, endured until the 1090s, when it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. How far this change involved wholesale population replacement is still debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> remained free of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a>, Gaelic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> control and was divided among Brittonic kingdoms, among them Gwynedd, Powys, Deheubarth, Gwent and Morgannwg. Cornwall was effectively annexed by the English from the mid 11th century. By the 11th century Brittonic speakers had split into the Welsh, Cornish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> and Cumbrians, and Common Brittonic had become Welsh, Cornish, Breton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>. Brittany was united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1532, and Wales with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the Laws in Wales Acts of 1535 to 1542.</p><p>Welsh and Breton survive, Cornish became extinct by the 19th century but has been revived since the 20th, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a> and Pictish died out in the 12th century. Most place names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, the Isles of Scilly and Brittany are Brittonic. The relationship of the Britons to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> is unclear, but most scholars accept that the Pictish language was related to Common Brittonic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Celtic Britons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Large-scale immigration under Wilfrid Laurier’s government after 1896, including Ukrainians, Germans, Scandinavians and Americans as well as <b>Britons</b>, settled the prairies, which became one of the world’s great wheat-growing regions, while Chinese labourers who had helped build the railway faced a head tax from 1885 and near-total exclusion…</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Military command passed to new officers, the Duke of the <b>Britons</b> and the Count of the Saxon Shore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Fans of Welsh football clubs and schools and colleges around the world sing their own words to it, and a comic parody, &quot;Woad&quot; or &quot;National Anthem of the <b>Ancient Britons</b>&quot;, was written by William Hope-Jones some time before 1914.</blockquote><a href="/songs/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a></li><li><blockquote>After their defeat Caesar made Commius their king and used him as an envoy to the <b>Britons</b> in 55 BCE, extending his authority over the Morini.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates">Atrebates</a></li><li><blockquote>Kent was the part of Britain closest to Gaul, and Caesar described its people as the most civilised of the <b>Britons</b>, much like the Gauls in custom.</blockquote><a href="/state/cantiaci">Cantiaci</a></li><li><blockquote>Cunobelinus, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from Camulodunum, which he took from the Trinovantes, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; Suetonius called him king of the <b>Britons</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li></ul></section>
